2025-04-08 14:25:21,068 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'headers': {'api-key': '<redacted>'}, 'files': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'content': '\nAssess the risk level of a customer based on the KYC report findings.\n\nCustomer Information:\n- Name: Bitfinex\n- Type: business\n- Business: Not provided\n\nReport Findings:\n## Company Information\n# Company Information: Bitfinex\n\n## 1. Key Findings from Research\n\nBitfinex is a cryptocurrency exchange founded in 2012, headquartered in Hong Kong. As one of the leading exchanges in the crypto sector, it offers services including trading of digital currencies and tokens, margin trading, and lending. The platform is particularly well known for allowing users to trade large amounts of cryptocurrencies, making it a popular choice among high-volume traders. Bitfinex was founded by Raphael Nicolle, and its operations have been supported by various stakeholders over the years.\n\n### History and Key Markets\nBitfinex initially began as a peer-to-peer Bitcoin exchange and expanded to include a variety of other cryptocurrencies. It serves a global clientele, with significant market activity in Asia, Europe, and North America according to various industry reports. Their business model is centered on providing a secure and efficient trading platform, though they have faced regulatory scrutiny in certain jurisdictions.\n\n- **Source:** Bitfinex Official Website ([bitfinex.com](https://www.bitfinex.com))\n- **Source:** CoinDesk Article on Bitfinex’s Founding ([coindesk.com](https://www.coindesk.com))\n\n## 2. Identified Risk Factors\n\nBitfinex has faced significant security and regulatory challenges in its history. Notably, in 2016, Bitfinex reported a security breach that resulted in the theft of approximately 120,000 BTC, highlighting vulnerabilities related to cryptocurrency exchange infrastructure ([source: The Verge](https://www.theverge.com)). Additionally, Bitfinex has had controversies regarding its relationship with Tether, a stablecoin issuer, and has faced scrutiny from regulators concerning the transparency and legality of its operations ([source: The New York Times](https://www.nytimes.com)).\n\nThese incidents suggest potential risks associated with financial crime, cybersecurity, and compliance with international financial regulations.\n\n## 3. Relevance to Company\'s Risk Appetite\n\nAccording to Corpay\'s Risk Appetite Statement, there is no appetite for customers involved in virtual currencies that are unauthorized, unlicensed, or unregulated exchanges. Bitfinex, as a prominent cryptocurrency exchange, may be subject to heightened scrutiny concerning financial crime risks including money laundering and fraud, given the decentralized and often anonymous nature of crypto transactions.\n\nBitfinex\'s history of regulation challenges and security breaches align with Corpay’s prohibitions against conducting business with entities that have repeated breaches of law, regulation, or policy applicable to financial crime risk. Hence, the business model and historical issues at Bitfinex may pose a heightened risk for Corpay’s compliance parameters.\n\n## 4. Supporting Evidence and Sources\n\n- **Security Breach Incident:** Reference to the 2016 breach and cryptocurrency theft ([source: The Verge](https://www.theverge.com))\n- **Regulatory Challenges:** Discussion of legal scrutiny over Tether relationship and operational transparency ([source: The New York Times](https://www.nytimes.com), [source: Financial Times](https://www.ft.com))\n\n## Conclusion\n\nBitfinex exhibits risk factors that are crucial to consider within the context of its cryptocurrency-based operations. While the exchange serves significant global markets, its historical security breaches and regulatory scrutiny present red flags that align with Corpay’s prohibitions and statements against entities at risk for financial crime. Enhanced due diligence is recommended before engaging with Bitfinex to ensure compliance with Corpay’s risk appetite and legal standards.\n\n## Business Activities Analysis\n# Business Activities Analysis\n\n## Customer Information\n- **Name:** Bitfinex\n- **Business:** Cryptocurrency exchange  \n\n## Key Findings from Research Related to This Section\n\nBitfinex is a well-established cryptocurrency exchange that has been operational since 2012. It offers various services, including cryptocurrency trading, margin trading, and lending. Bitfinex has been involved in notable industry events, such as the 2016 hack where approximately 120,000 Bitcoins were stolen, and its association with the stablecoin Tether, which has faced scrutiny over its reserve practices.\n\n### Sources:\n- [CoinDesk](https://www.coindesk.com/information/2016/08/02/overview-of-the-bitfinex-hack/)\n- [Reuters](https://www.reuters.com/technology/cryptocurrency-firm-tether-has-faced-backlash-heres-why-2021-06-03/)\n\n## Identified Risk Factors\n\n1. **Regulatory Scrutiny:** Bitfinex has faced regulatory challenges, particularly related to its partnership with Tether. Regulatory scrutiny may impact its operational compliance and financial stability.\n\n2. **Security Concerns:** Following the significant hack in 2016, Bitfinex implemented stronger security measures. However, past security breaches remain a concern for potential vulnerabilities.\n\n3. **Market Volatility:** As a cryptocurrency exchange, Bitfinex operates within a highly volatile market, which may expose it to substantial financial risks and liquidity concerns.\n\n4. **Compliance History:** There are concerns about Bitfinex\'s compliance practices, stemming from investigations and regulatory fines.\n\n### Sources:\n- [Bloomberg](https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2021-07-29/tether-and-bitfinex-ordered-by-new-york-to-stop-trading-in-city)\n- [The Verge](https://www.theverge.com/2021/10/15/22728358/bitfinex-crypto-exchange-tether-lawsuit-settlement)\n\n## Relevance to Company\'s Risk Appetite\n\nBitfinex\'s business activities align with Corpay\'s risk appetite statement concerning virtual currencies and regulated exchanges. However, its involvement in regulatory scrutiny, historical security breaches, and compliance challenges presents heightened risk factors that require meticulous due diligence.\n\nCorpay explicitly prohibits engagements with unregulated exchanges and entities involved with illegal activities, emphasizing the importance of enhanced due diligence in such cases.\n\n## Supporting Evidence and Sources\n\nGiven Bitfinex\'s history of regulatory scrutiny and past security incidents, there is substantial evidence requiring Corpay to perform comprehensive due diligence before any engagement. The identification of these risk factors highlights areas where Bitfinex\'s practices must be verified against Corpay\'s risk appetite statement.\n\n### Sources:\n- [Financial Times](https://www.ft.com/content/131b8931-897f-4b91-ae7e-7ad5ad6d1134)\n- [CNN Business](https://www.cnn.com/2020/09/23/investing/bitfinex-cryptocurrency-issues/index.html)\n\n## Conclusion\n\nBitfinex presents several risk factors tied to security and regulatory compliance. While offering legitimate cryptocurrency services, its history necessitates vigilant due diligence to ensure compliance with Corpay’s risk appetite. Enhanced examination of Bitfinex\'s operational integrity and regulatory adherence is crucial before considering any collaboration, affirming Corpay\'s commitment to lawful and reputable business engagements.\n\n## Regulatory Compliance and Licensing\n## Regulatory Compliance and Licensing\n\n### 1. Key Findings from Research\n\nBitfinex is a cryptocurrency exchange platform part of iFinex Inc., reportedly registered in the British Virgin Islands. The company is known to operate its services globally, but it has faced scrutiny and regulatory actions in multiple jurisdictions. In particular, the New York Attorney General (NYAG) took legal action against Bitfinex in 2019 due to concerns over the loss of access to customer funds and failure to maintain adequate controls over its accounts and operations. \n\nAdditionally, Bitfinex has not been able to secure regulatory licenses in significant markets such as the United States, where stringent requirements regarding anti-money laundering (AML) and countering the financing of terrorism (CFT) apply. Without such licenses, Bitfinex is restricted from directly offering services to U.S. residents. \n\n**Sources:**\n- New York Attorney General Press Release [(April 2019)](https://ag.ny.gov)\n- Bitfinex website (bitfinex.com) \n- CoinDesk Article on Regulatory Actions [(2021)](https://www.coindesk.com)\n\n### 2. Identified Risk Factors\n\n- **Jurisdictional Risks**: Operating primarily from the British Virgin Islands, a known offshore financial center, Bitfinex faces heightened regulatory scrutiny, particularly regarding financial transparency and compliance with global AML norms.\n  \n- **Licensing Gaps**: The absence of regulatory licenses in major jurisdictions like the United States presents risks related to legal consequences and potential operational disruptions.\n\n- **Historical Legal Issues**: Past legal actions by regulatory bodies, such as that from NYAG, suggest potential vulnerabilities in Bitfinex\'s adherence to compliance obligations.\n\n- **Association with High-risk Entities**: The cryptocurrency sector, by nature, involves certain elevated risks, such as money laundering and fraud.\n\n### 3. Relevance to Company\'s Risk Appetite\n\nCorpay\'s Risk Appetite Statement categorically outlines a lack of appetite for unregulated entities and those involved in financial crimes, which includes unauthorized or unlicensed exchanges of virtual currencies. Bitfinex\'s operations, particularly its prior entanglements with the NYAG and lack of U.S. licensing, place it in a category that would necessitate enhanced due diligence. The connection to a high-risk industry such as virtual asset service providing would typically require exhaustive compliance verification, which might not align with Corpay\'s stated risk tolerances.\n\n### 4. Supporting Evidence and Sources\n\n- **NYAG Legal Action (2019)**: The legal document accuses Bitfinex of covering up nearly $850 million in losses by using funds from its affiliated company, Tether. This forms a critical aspect of its compliance history, citing operational opacity and financial mismanagement. [(NYAG](https://ag.ny.gov))\n  \n- **License Status**: Bitfinex continues to face hurdles in acquiring licenses in strictly regulated regions, such as the U.S., thereby demonstrating gaps in compliance preparedness.\n\n- **Industry Risks**: According to the Financial Action Task Force (FATF), virtual asset service providers often encounter challenges in meeting international regulatory standards, including customer identification and transaction monitoring. \n\n### Conclusion\n\nBitfinex exhibits several regulatory compliance concerns that could represent significant risk factors in alignment with Corpay\'s risk appetite framework. Without recognized licenses in key jurisdictions and a history of legal disputes, the company poses potential compliance and reputational risks. This analysis should guide decision-making concerning business engagements with Bitfinex, particularly given the stringent regulatory posture Corpay maintains concerning financial crime and oversight requirements. \n\n**Actionable Recommendation**: Consider conducting an in-depth review of Bitfinex\'s current compliance frameworks and a focused analysis on any changes post-NYAG settlement before proceeding with any business relationship.\n\n## AML/KYC Practices\n# AML/KYC Practices\n\n## Key Findings\n\nBitfinex is a cryptocurrency exchange platform established for trading various virtual assets. As of the latest assessment, Bitfinex has implemented several measures to comply with AML (Anti-Money Laundering) and KYC regulations, including customer identification processes, transaction monitoring, and regular audits. However, being part of the virtual currency industry, it inherently presents certain risks that align with Corpay’s restrictions and prohibitions on unregulated exchanges. Bitfinex is known to take customer security seriously and deploys advanced security measures to protect user data and assets ([source](https://www.bitfinex.com/security)).\n\n## Identified Risk Factors\n\n- **Virtual Asset Services Provider:** Bitfinex operates in the virtual asset space, a category of heightened concern under Corpay’s risk appetite statement due to potential risks associated with money laundering and terrorism financing.\n- **Regulatory Uncertainty:** The regulatory landscape for cryptocurrency exchanges is evolving, which presents risks related to compliance with AML/KYC regulations. Historical incidents such as legal challenges ([source](https://www.coindesk.com/)) suggest potential vulnerabilities.\n- **Anonymous Numbered Accounts:** While Bitfinex reportedly complies with KYC processes including verification, the anonymity affiliated with cryptocurrencies remains a risk factor.\n- **Historical Data Breaches:** Bitfinex has experienced security breaches in the past, raising concerns about its ability to protect transactions and store valued customer data ([source](https://www.reuters.com/)).\n\n## Relevance to Company\'s Risk Appetite\n\nBitfinex, operating in the virtual currency space, closely aligns with several concerns outlined in Corpay\'s risk appetite statement. It falls under the category of a Virtual Asset Service Provider, which requires enhanced due diligence to mitigate any financial crime risks. Cryptocurrency exchanges are identified as potential conduits for illicit activities due to the decentralized and often anonymous nature of transactions. \n\n## Supporting Evidence and Sources\n\n1. **Security Practices:** Bitfinex claims robust security protocols, including multi-signature wallets and dual-factor authentication ([Bitfinex Security Page](https://www.bitfinex.com/security)).\n2. **Regulatory Challenges:** Several legal challenges and investigations have been associated with Bitfinex, affecting its operation and compliance measures ([CoinDesk](https://www.coindesk.com/business/2021-control-chainalysis-bitfinex-market-security)).\n3. **Past Incidents:** Security breaches have been reported, notably a hack in 2016, resulting in the loss of 119,756 BTC ([Reuters](https://www.reuters.com/article/us-bitfinex-breach-idUSKCN10H1M6)).\n\n## Conclusion\n\nDespite adopting certain AML/KYC measures, Bitfinex presents several risk factors that align with Corpay’s heightened concerns in the virtual currency sector. The exchange\'s involvement in past security breaches and regulatory challenges poses significant risks that require comprehensive evaluation and enhanced due diligence. It is imperative for any engagement with Bitfinex to maintain stringent compliance oversight and continual risk assessment to ensure adherence to Corpay’s risk appetite and regulatory requirements.\n\n## Negative News and Regulatory Actions\n# Negative News and Regulatory Actions\n\n## Customer Information\n- **Name**: Bitfinex\n- **Business**: Cryptocurrency Exchange (assumed based on context)\n\n## Key Findings from Research\n\nBitfinex has been involved in several controversies and regulatory actions over the years, particularly in relation to its operation as a cryptocurrency exchange. The key findings from the research include:\n\n1. **Legal and Regulatory Investigations**: \n   - The New York Attorney General (NYAG) conducted an investigation that led to a legal battle regarding allegations that Bitfinex attempted to cover up the loss of $850 million by using funds from Tether, a sister company. In 2021, Bitfinex and Tether reached an $18.5 million settlement with the NYAG without admitting any wrongdoing. [Source: New York Attorney General Press Release]\n   - Bitfinex has faced multiple subpoenas and inquiries from U.S. regulators regarding its business practices and compliance with federal laws.\n   \n2. **Operational Concerns**:\n   - Allegations of facilitating money laundering and tax evasion have been leveled against Bitfinex, although no formal charges have been brought.\n   - Bitfinex has had operational disruptions due to hacking incidents, notably a high-profile hack in 2016 where 120,000 BTC were stolen. [Source: Forbes]\n\n## Identified Risk Factors\n\n- **Regulatory Scrutiny**: Bitfinex’s regulatory challenges in the United States highlight significant risk, particularly concerning compliance with financial crime regulations.\n- **Cybersecurity Risks**: The past incidences of security breaches suggest ongoing vulnerabilities in its platform, raising concerns about data and asset security.\n- **Financial Practices and Transparency**: The opaque nature of its financial dealings, as evidenced by the NYAG settlement, poses risks related to fraud detection and financial integrity.\n\n## Relevance to Company’s Risk Appetite\n\nBitfinex’s operations and historical controversies are notably at odds with Corpay’s risk appetite statement, which highlights the following prohibitions relevant to Bitfinex\'s profile:\n\n- **Virtual Currencies**: Bitfinex operates as a cryptocurrency exchange, a category identified as high risk due to unauthorized, unlicensed, or unregulated exchanges.\n- **Money Laundering**: Allegations and investigations into Bitfinex’s financial practices align with areas Corpay seeks to avoid.\n  \nCorpay’s restrictions on operations involving virtual asset service providers further indicate potential incompatibility, necessitating enhanced due diligence.\n\n## Supporting Evidence and Sources\n\n- **New York Attorney General Press Release** outlining the settlement agreement and investigation findings.  \n- **Forbes Article on Bitfinex Hack** providing details on the security breaches and cyber threats.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe negative news and regulatory actions associated with Bitfinex present significant risks that conflict with Corpay’s specified risk parameters. While Bitfinex continues to operate within various jurisdictions, the history of legal challenges and cybersecurity issues constitutes a considerable concern. Enhanced due diligence and review of Bitfinex’s compliance with international financial regulations are recommended before any engagement, in line with Corpay’s risk profile and appetite.\n\n## Technical and Cybersecurity Risk\n# Technical and Cybersecurity Risk Assessment of Bitfinex\n\n## 1. Key Findings from Research\n\nBitfinex is a prominent virtual asset service provider (VASP) that offers cryptocurrency exchange services. Known for its advanced trading platform, Bitfinex has a solid reputation in the cryptocurrency market. However, it has faced challenges in the past, particularly related to security breaches. Some key incidents include:\n\n- **2016 Security Breach**: Bitfinex experienced a major security breach in August 2016, which resulted in the theft of approximately 120,000 BTC, valued at roughly $72 million at the time (source: Coindesk, 2016).\n- **Cybersecurity Enhancements**: Following the breach, Bitfinex implemented substantial security enhancements, including increased user protection measures like two-factor authentication and multi-signature wallets (source: Bitfinex Blog, 2016).\n  \n## 2. Identified Risk Factors\n\n- **Historical Security Breaches**: The 2016 hack raises concerns about Bitfinex\'s ability to effectively safeguard digital assets against cyber threats.\n- **Regulatory Risks**: As a VASP, Bitfinex operates in a highly regulated environment, requiring stringent compliance with global and local regulations on anti-money laundering (AML) and combating the financing of terrorism (CFT).\n- **Technical Infrastructure**: Given the volatile nature of cryptocurrencies, technical robustness and resilience against cyberattacks are paramount. Any vulnerabilities in Bitfinex\'s systems could pose significant operational risks.\n\n## 3. Relevance to Company\'s Risk Appetite\n\nBased on Corpay\'s risk appetite statement, Bitfinex\'s operations as a virtual asset service provider fall under industries with heightened scrutiny. Corpay shows no appetite for engaging with unauthorized, unlicensed, or unregulated exchanges. While Bitfinex is a legitimate exchange, the historical security breaches and the inherent risks of the cryptocurrency industry necessitate a cautious approach:\n\n- **Heightened Due Diligence**: It is crucial for Corpay to perform enhanced due diligence on Bitfinex to ensure compliance with financial crime risk protocols and to assess technical safeguards against potential cyber threats.\n- **Verification of Compliance**: Verifying Bitfinex\'s compliance with international standards and regulations, including those for virtual asset service providers, is vital to align with Corpay\'s risk restrictiveness.\n\n## 4. Supporting Evidence and Sources\n\n- Coindesk (2016), Reporting on Bitfinex Hack: [Link](https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2016/08/03/119756-bitcoins-stolen-from-bitfinex-in-largest-theft-since-mt-gox/)\n- Bitfinex Blog (2016), Security Enhancements Post-Hack: [Link](https://blog.bitfinex.com/post-hack-security-enhancements/)\n- Official statements and announcements from Bitfinex related to cybersecurity and infrastructure improvements can be found on their corporate blog and press releases.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nWhile Bitfinex has demonstrated a commitment to improving its cybersecurity measures after the 2016 breach, the historical security incident and the inherent risks associated with the cryptocurrency exchange industry necessitate ongoing monitoring and rigorous compliance verification for alignment with Corpay\'s risk appetite. Enhanced due diligence should be a continuous process to ensure adherence to financial and cybersecurity regulations, promoting a mutually secure business relationship.\n\nCompany Risk Appetite Statement:\n\nCorpay Risk Appetite Statement\n\nProhibitions\n\nCorpay has no appetite for customers who engage in any of the following:\n\nIntentional or willfully negligent breaches of law, regulation, or policy applicable to Financial Crime Risk.\nRepeated unintentional or repeated accidental breaches of law, regulation, or policy applicable to Financial Crime Risk.\nMisusing as account for the purposes of money laundering or terrorism financing.\nFacilitating business activities which could be construed as a tax offence.\nRefusing to provide sufficient information or documentation to demonstrate compliance with the standards outlined in this statement.\nThe Company intends to conduct business only with reputable customers who use their own products, services, and related accounts for legitimate purposes, and whose identities can be determined and verified. In keeping with that principle, the Company will not knowingly conduct business with clients that seek to process payments through Corpay involving:\n\n \nIllegal or illicit activities, including:\no Dealers involved in the distribution of arms and munitions\no Unlawful drugs (including cannabis industry)\no Red light businesses/adult entertainment\no Human trafficking\no Human exploitation\no Unlicensed and unlawful gambling\nVirtual Currencies: unauthorized, unlicensed or unregulated exchanges\nUnlicensed, unregistered and / or unregulated MSBs\nAnonymous numbered accounts\nPayable through accounts\nUnlicensed banks\nShell banks\nOn-line gambling entities: For U.S. Dollar clearing accounts, transactions that are restricted or unlawful under the Unlawful Internet Gambling Enforcement Act (UIGEA) and Regulation GG of the Federal Reserve Board, 12 C.F.R. Part 233, Corpay understands that online gambling is not illegal in certain countries; however, Corpay is citing a U.S. regulation that prohibits these flows through U.S. Dollar accounts.\nRestrictions\nCorpay has heightened concerns about the risk presented by the following types of industries and will look to restrict activities which it deems suspicious in nature. For these categories, Corpay Compliance will perform enhanced due diligence and may reach out to the applicable front office employee and/or the prospective customer, to better understand transactional flows:\n\n \nArms, defense, military;\nCall-Ins (at least and until it can be determined that the risk of fraud/identity theft has been \nremoved).\nCharities and other not-for-profit organizations;\nCash-intensive businesses, the type of which have known to have been used for money laundering and/or the financing of terror (including, for example, restaurants, bars and nightclubs, laundromats);\nDealers in high value/high portability luxury goods (such as dealers in diamonds and precious stones, precious metals, furs, art, boats, planes, vehicles, art dealers, including auctioneers dealing in any such goods);\nEmbassies/ consulates;\nEntities operating under offshore banking licenses\nFinancial Services Providers or "Gatekeepers" including Money Services Businesses and other fiduciaries or intermediaries (including businesses that feature credit and stored value cards, smartcards, e-cash, and/or online banking, ecommerce/online retail businesses, payment service providers (PSPs), and including such professionals as Lawyers, Accountants, Securities Brokers/Dealers, Real Estate Brokers/Dealers, and Financial Advisors);\nGambling entities (excluding state sponsored lotteries);\nGeneral Trading Companies;\nCannabis-related businesses;\nMulti-level Marketing Companies or Direct Sellers;\nPolitically Exposed Persons and Heads of International Organizations (HIOs);\nTravel and tour operators;\nThird Party Payment Processors;\nUsed Vehicle Dealers;\nVirtual Asset Service Providers; and\nWhere beneficial ownership information cannot be confirmed.\nCorpay employees are expected to properly identify these industries during the prospecting and onboarding \nprocesses.\n\n\nPlease analyze the information and determine:\n1. The primary business activity (from the BusinessActivity enum)\n2. Any prohibited activities (from the ProhibitedActivity enum)\n3. Whether the customer is politically exposed\n4. Whether beneficial ownership is confirmed\n5. The overall risk category (LOW_RISK, MEDIUM_RISK, HIGH_RISK, or PROHIBITED)\n6. A compliance score between 0.0 and 1.0\n7. Additional risk factors (as a dictionary)\n8. Decision reasoning and recommended actions\n\nOutput in JSON format compatible with the CustomerRiskAssessmentModel.\n', 'role': 'user'}], 'model': None, 'stream': False, 'tool_choice': {'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'CustomerRiskAssessmentModel'}}, 'tools': [{'type': 'function', 'function': {'name': 'CustomerRiskAssessmentModel', 'description': 'Comprehensive KYC risk assessment model', 'parameters': {'properties': {'customer_name': {'description': 'Name of the customer or business', 'type': 'string'}, 'customer_type': {'description': 'Type of customer (individual/business)', 'type': 'string'}, 'overall_risk_category': {'description': "Enumeration of risk categories based on Corpay's risk appetite", 'enum': ['low_risk', 'medium_risk', 'high_risk', 'prohibited'], 'type': 'string'}, 'primary_business_activity': {'anyOf': [{'description': 'Enumeration of business activities with associated risk', 'enum': ['arms_defense', 'call_ins', 'charities', 'cash_intensive', 'luxury_goods', 'embassies', 'offshore_banking', 'financial_services', 'gambling', 'general_trading', 'cannabis', 'multi_level_marketing', 'politically_exposed', 'travel_tours', 'third_party_processors', 'used_vehicle_dealers', 'virtual_asset_providers', 'other'], 'title': 'BusinessActivity', 'type': 'string'}, {'type': 'null'}], 'default': None, 'description': 'Primary business activity of the customer'}, 'prohibited_activities': {'description': 'List of prohibited activities associated with the customer', 'items': {'description': 'Enumeration of explicitly prohibited activities', 'enum': ['arms_distribution', 'unlawful_drugs', 'adult_entertainment', 'human_trafficking', 'human_exploitation', 'unlawful_gambling', 'unauthorized_virtual_currencies', 'unlicensed_msb', 'anonymous_accounts', 'shell_banks'], 'type': 'string'}, 'type': 'array'}, 'is_politically_exposed': {'default': False, 'description': 'Whether the customer is a Politically Exposed Person (PEP)', 'type': 'boolean'}, 'beneficial_ownership_confirmed': {'default': False, 'description': 'Whether beneficial ownership information has been confirmed', 'type': 'boolean'}, 'risk_factors': {'description': 'Additional risk factors and their details', 'type': 'object'}, 'compliance_score': {'default': 0.0, 'description': 'Compliance score (0.0 - 1.0)', 'maximum': 1.0, 'minimum': 0.0, 'type': 'number'}, 'additional_notes': {'anyOf': [{'type': 'string'}, {'type': 'null'}], 'default': None, 'description': 'Additional notes or comments about the assessment'}}, 'required': ['customer_name', 'customer_type'], 'type': 'object'}}}]}}
